  so yes so this is two representative  compounds for a program in ALS so ALS is  a mullah terrific lateral sclerosis also  known as Lou Gehrig's disease to many of  you it is a neurodegenerative disorder  and so we were able to take these  compounds and optimize their properties  to make drugs in a very short amount of  time so less than three months versus  the industry average of about a year and  a half and so the properties that we  optimized affinity so how tightly does  it bind is it soluble and then the last  one is called its ability to enter the  brain so this is a neurodegenerative  disorder so the compound has an  additional challenge it has to get back  get past what's called you a blood-brain  barrier which is a protective mechanism  so that's actually makes things even  more difficult so there's been external  demonstrations just recently as well so  there's a validation of compounds that  have been optimized into drugs by  machine learning and they found five  highly active drugs that have been  produced and so lastly sort of just to  conclude measuring models and  benchmarking our models is extremely  important to us and it's it's fairly  challenging in this case with generative  models do they give you something good  it's fairly easy to get a qualitative  read sometimes if the model isn't doing  so well you get a very explicit  qualitative read from your chemists in  the in in certain words that they're not  happy with but but how do you how do you  really measure this and so we've come up  with a way that we believe is a fair and  sort of balanced way of measuring these  generative models and we look at two  bacteria so can the model reproduce a  broad distribution of of chemical space  so what you saw with that teasing  embedding so how well can you reproduce  whatever chemical universe you given so  there's a variety of metrics that we've  posed in what we've developed as a  benchmarking suite the second one though  is that the general models are usually  really good at being general models but  they're not great once you start  optimizing them for a very specific  question  so does that model then also adapt or  are you able to get it to to to drive a  specific multi parameter optimization  and so how well then do you really focus  in on a specific area of space that  you're interested in and so we call this  benchmarking suite guacomole and we've  published recently a paper on it and  there is code up on get lab or github  excuse me  so with that end with that I would like  to thank you so these are some of the  people who've helped out with this work  as well so I'm available today and  tomorrow if you want to talk  you
 

